// @check
// @error operator `-` takes 2 parameters (self, other), got 1 — operator dispatch is binary only, so this declaration could never fire
// @error operator `+` takes 2 parameters (self, other), got 1 — operator dispatch is binary only, so this declaration could never fire
// @error operator `*` takes 2 parameters (self, other), got 0 — operator dispatch is binary only, so this declaration could never fire
// @error operator `/` takes 2 parameters (self, other), got 3 — operator dispatch is binary only, so this declaration could never fire
// GRADUATED from `silent-class-inventory` D444 — the third shape of the reject
// `error-equality-not-overloadable.vl` (D46) and `index/operator-arity.vl` already
// carry, and the one that was silent.
//
// A ONE-PARAMETER OPERATOR DECLARATION WAS INERT AT EVERY RECEIVER. `function
// -(self: i32): i32 { return 99 }` + `print(-a)` was `vl check` rc 0 and printed
// `-7`: the declaration's own body says the answer is 99 and nothing dispatched to
// it. That is strictly stronger than D425, where the receiver decides — here no
// `self` type makes it work, because there is no unary dispatch path in the tree at
// all. `checkUnaryNode` handles `!`, `p+`/`p-` and `-`, and for `-` asks only
// `isNumeric(arg)`; it never looks for an operator function. `opSelfFnTy` cannot
// answer either — it returns -1 unless `d.fnParams.length == 2`.
//
// AND THE LINTER BELIEVED IT DISPATCHED. `compiler/lint.vl` said in its own words
// "An operator method (`-x` → `function -(self)`) dispatches by operator name" and
// added `n.unOp` to the reference set on that belief, so the dead declaration was
// not even reported as unused. Three components disagreed about whether the feature
// existed; that comment is corrected in the same change.
//
// EVERY ARITY BUT 2, not just 1 — the row filed the one-parameter case and the
// measurement widened it. `function *(): i32` and `function /(self, b, c)` declared
// just as cleanly and did just as little. The predicate is `params.length != 2`,
// which is what `opSelfFnTy` requires, so the reject's boundary is the dispatch's
// boundary by construction.
//
// REJECTED RATHER THAN IMPLEMENTED, on D46's own test: who is the customer? Nothing
// in the tree, in `std`, or in the 2,652-cell distilled corpus declares an operator
// at any arity but 2 — the reject costs no capability. Building unary dispatch
// instead would be a new language feature (a `checkUnaryNode` arm plus an emitter
// path plus a rep decision for `-x` over a struct), and the row is not evidence that
// anyone wants one; it is evidence that the compiler currently accepts a name no
// reference could ever be written for. `-` is not an identifier, so no call can be
// spelled, and the quoted form mints the identical name — the exact thing
// `index/operator-quoted-name-not-an-operator.vl` refuses for `function "shout"`.
//
// THE BOUNDARY IS ARITY, NEVER THE RECEIVER, and the controls below say so at both
// ends. When this file landed, an arity-2 declaration over a BUILT-IN receiver still
// declared clean and was still silently ignored — that was D425, filed open and
// thought blocked behind D402, and this gate deliberately did not touch it.
// D425 CLOSED on 2026-08-28 (`error-operator-dead-self-type.vl`), so the built-in
// receiver is now refused by ITS gate, at the hoist, and the 40 `d425c*` cells that
// were this file's refutation pin have become the record of a price PAID. The
// controls here are therefore both arity-2 over a STRUCT, which is what this gate
// was always about: arity decides, and a well-formed arity-2 declaration is somebody
// else's question.
type V = { x: i32 }

function -(self: i32): i32 { return 99 }

function "+"(self: V): i32 { return 99 }

function *(): i32 { return 99 }

function /(self: V, b: V, c: V): i32 { return 99 }

// CONTROLS — neither of these is this gate's business.
// An arity-2 operator over a struct DISPATCHES and must keep dispatching.
function <(self: V, b: V): boolean { return self.x < b.x }

// An arity-2 operator over a STRUCT is accepted by this gate AND by D425's, which is
// the pair of answers that makes the two gates disjoint. (It was `self: i32` until
// D425 closed; that spelling is now refused by the other gate, and leaving it here
// would have made this file assert a reject it does not own.)
function %(self: V, b: V): i32 { return 99 }

function cell(a: V, b: V): boolean { a < b }

print(cell({ x: 1 }, { x: 2 }))
